Here is a clean Template Method implementation in Java. The template `sort()` defines the skeleton and the primitive operations `compare` and `swap` are provided by the base class. The concrete algorithms `BubbleSort` and `SelectionSort` implement the algorithm-specific `doSort()` step.

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

abstract class SortAlgorithm<T extends Comparable<T>> {
    protected T[] array;

    public SortAlgorithm(T[] array) {
        this.array = array;
    }

    // Template Method - final skeleton
    public final void sort() {
        if (array == null || array.length < 2) return;
        doSort();               // algorithm specific hook
        onSorted();
    }

    // Primitive operations - can be overridden if needed
    protected int compare(T a, T b) {
        return a.compareTo(b);
    }

    protected void swap(int i, int j) {
        T tmp = array[i];
        array[i] = array[j];
        array[j] = tmp;
    }

    // Hook for subclasses
    protected void onSorted() {}

    // Abstract primitive for the algorithm steps
    protected abstract void doSort();

    public T[] getArray() {
        return array;
    }
}

class BubbleSort<T extends Comparable<T>> extends SortAlgorithm<T> {
    public BubbleSort(T[] array) {
        super(array);
    }

    @Override
    protected void doSort() {
        int n = array.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            boolean swapped = false;
            for (int j = 0; j < n - 1 - i; j++) {
                if (compare(array[j], array[j + 1]) > 0) {
                    swap(j, j + 1);
                    swapped = true;
                }
            }
            if (!swapped) break;
        }
    }
}

class SelectionSort<T extends Comparable<T>> extends SortAlgorithm<T> {
    public SelectionSort(T[] array) {
        super(array);
    }

    @Override
    protected void doSort() {
        int n = array.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            int minIdx = i;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                if (compare(array[j], array[minIdx]) < 0) {
                    minIdx = j;
                }
            }
            if (minIdx != i) {
                swap(i, minIdx);
            }
        }
    }
}

public class TemplateMethodSortDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Integer[] nums = {5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6};
        String[] words = {"pear", "apple", "orange", "banana"};

        SortAlgorithm<Integer> bubbleInt = new BubbleSort<>(nums.clone());
        bubbleInt.sort();
        System.out.println("BubbleSort ints: " + Arrays.toString(bubbleInt.getArray()));

        SortAlgorithm<String> selectStr = new SelectionSort<>(words.clone());
        selectStr.sort();
        System.out.println("SelectionSort strings: " + Arrays.toString(selectStr.getArray()));

        SortAlgorithm<Integer> selectInt = new SelectionSort<>(nums.clone());
        selectInt.sort();
        System.out.println("SelectionSort ints: " + Arrays.toString(selectInt.getArray()));

        SortAlgorithm<String> bubbleStr = new BubbleSort<>(words.clone());
        bubbleStr.sort();
        System.out.println("BubbleSort strings: " + Arrays.toString(bubbleStr.getArray()));
    }
}
```

The `SortAlgorithm.sort()` template controls the overall workflow, while `BubbleSort` and `SelectionSort` supply their own `doSort()` implementation using the shared `compare()` and `swap()` primitives. This keeps the algorithm skeleton stable and the varying steps interchangeable.
